  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Suppose $a<b$. Construct a bijection to show that $\\mathbb R$ and $(a,b)$ have the same cardinality.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f:(a,b)\\to\\mathbb R$, $f(x)=\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi(x-a)}{b-a}-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nThe affine argument maps $(a,b)$ bijectively onto $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2})$, where tangent is a bijection onto $\\mathbb R$.",
      "worked": "Define $g:(a,b)\\to(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2})$ by\n$$g(x)=\\frac{\\pi(x-a)}{b-a}-\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$$\nSince $b-a>0$, $g$ is strictly increasing and maps $(a,b)$ onto $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2})$. Tangent is strictly increasing on this interval and has range $\\mathbb R$. Hence the composition\n$$f:(a,b)\\to\\mathbb R,\\qquad f(x)=\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi(x-a)}{b-a}-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$$\nis injective and surjective, so it is a bijection. Therefore $|\\mathbb R|=|(a,b)|$.\n\nIts inverse gives a bijection in the opposite direction:\n$$f^{-1}(y)=a+\\frac{b-a}{\\pi}\\left(\\arctan y+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right),\\qquad y\\in\\mathbb R.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
